__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.venv/
venv/

# Corpora and tokenized streams -- regenerate with scripts/prepare_*.py
data/*
# ...except the two pools that are *written* rather than generated. The rule
# above says "regenerate", and these two cannot be: `docs/VOICE.md`'s pool
# rule is that his speech is real or hand-written and never assembled, so the
# self family's 205 lines (arm 9) and the open family's 144 (arm 11) are
# source in the way a generator is source. Losing them to a fresh clone would
# make both arms unreproducible and both payloads unrewritable. They carry no
# corpus, no personal data and no name -- the shipped name is a `{name}` tag
# and `scripts/check_social.py` asserts it. Spelt as a re-descent (`data/*`,
# then `data/raw/*`) because git does not look inside an ignored *directory*,
# so a bare `data/` above would make the two negations below dead lines.
!data/raw/
data/raw/*
!data/raw/self_replies.txt
!data/raw/open_replies.txt
# A3b's two hand-written pools (configs/mix_v3_a3b.py): the wider user side
# and the replies for share, farewell, consolation and open's residual --
# source in the same sense, no corpus, no name, no personal data.
!data/raw/move_carriers.txt
!data/raw/move_replies.txt

# Paraphrase arms of the frozen eval set -- regenerate with
# scripts/paraphrase_evals.py. Unlike `evals/`, these are not frozen but
# *derived*: a pure deterministic function of committed inputs (`evals/`, the
# pools in the script, a module-constant seed), verified byte-identical across
# a rebuild. The sha256 of every file is embedded in each scorecard under
# `manifest.files`, so a rebuild that drifted would fail against any result
# already written -- which is the guarantee committing them would have bought,
# without 10MB of text duplicated out of `evals/`.
evals_para/
evals_retemplate/
evals_heldout/
evals_wideonly/

# Payload arms -- same argument, same guarantee, `scripts/payload_evals.py`.
# These rewrite the observation rather than the utterance.
evals_payload_reorder/
evals_payload_extra_key/
evals_payload_renamed/
evals_payload_nested/
evals_payload_phrasing/
evals_payload_phrasing2/
# The seventh moves `say` rather than `clarify`, so it is the only one that
# rewrites `say.jsonl` and `namecollide.jsonl` -- and the only one whose
# untouched files are a genuine invariance control.
evals_payload_say_phrasing/

# Tool-name arms -- same argument again, `scripts/newtool_evals.py`. These move
# the name inside `<|call|>` and nothing else, so `prompt_call` is untouched and
# the four routing rows are their invariance control.
evals_newtool_newtool/
evals_newtool_swapname/

# Checkpoints
checkpoints/
*.pt

# The guard sweep's per-guard timings, appended by every unfiltered
# `scripts/check_all.py` run and read back to name a guard that doubled. They
# are this machine's numbers under whatever else was on the card, so a copy on
# another machine would compare against a stranger's hardware.
guard_times.jsonl

# The runtime's day log. `corn/agent.py` appends one line per `save_note` and
# `recall_day` reads it back, so it is per-machine state written by running the
# agent -- not an artefact of the project. Committed once by accident during the
# note-payload session and removed in the same commit.
notes.jsonl
# The escalation queue. `corn/agent.py:_ask_agent` appends one line per
# `ask_agent` call and the request text is whatever the person said, so this is
# per-machine state on exactly the terms notes.jsonl and contacts.json are.
# It first appeared during P3-B2's searchgrid runs, when both fine-tuned arms
# reached `ask_agent` on a handful of turns.
escalations.jsonl
# The schedule store. `harness/backends/schedule.py` holds actions that fire
# later -- reminders, texts waiting on their time -- and both the message text
# and the recipients are the person's own. Same terms as the three above.
schedule.jsonl
# The agent ledger. `harness/backends/relay.py` records every escalation the
# transport actually sent and everything that came back -- the user's own
# requests and a frontier model's replies to them. VISION §6 declares it
# memory, which is exactly why it stays on this machine.
agent_ledger.jsonl
# Which day he last mentioned an occasion on. `corn/persona.py:Marker` writes
# one line so a server restarted at lunchtime on his birthday does not wish
# himself many happy returns a second time. Per-machine state written by
# running the agent, on exactly the terms the four files above are.
persona_state.json
# Data packs. `harness/backends/packs.py` answers out of whatever CSVs and
# text the owner drops in here -- their game stats, their recipes, their
# reading list. The shipped artefact stays user-neutral because this folder
# is where an instance gets to be somebody's.
packs/
# The runtime contact book -- real people, and VISION §4's rule is the reason:
# the shipped artefact is user-neutral and a running instance is one person's
# permanently. A contact book is the second kind of thing and must never reach
# a commit. See harness/backends/contacts.py.
contacts.json

# Transcripts of actual conversations, written by `/saved` in `harness/client.py`
# (and until now by copy-pasting the terminal). Same rule as contacts.json and
# notes.jsonl: what a running instance accumulates belongs to the person running
# it, not to the artefact everyone else clones.
Saved Sessions/

# `scripts/score_pipeline.py` writes one stdout log per stage per queue into a
# timestamped directory here. They are a transcript of a run rather than a
# result -- every number worth keeping is in the scorecard the same run wrote
# to `evals/results/` -- and they accumulate a dozen files per queue. Ignored
# rather than deleted, so a failure is still readable in the working tree while
# the pipeline's live path is new enough to be worth watching.
harness/results/logs/

# Where the overlay window was last dragged to -- this machine, not the repo.
overlay.json

# The voice client's tuning -- harness/voice/config.py says it belongs here
# beside overlay.json, and until the studio session it was only said.
voice.json

# The server's configuration -- harness/serverconfig.py. Which checkpoint,
# whose packs, which tools may run: per-machine state on overlay.json's terms,
# written by hand or by the studio's Config tab.
server.json

# Declaration-file tools -- harness/backends/toolsd.py. Each file names a
# command on *this* machine and what the owner lets it do, which is contacts.json's
# rule: a running instance is one person's, and their wiring never reaches a commit.
tools.d/

# Stray probe and boot logs written beside the repo root by a session that
# redirected a server's stdout there. Nothing reads them and nothing should:
# a card's `source.dirty` is how a scorecard says which side of a commit it
# was taken on (harness/results/README.md), and an untracked scratch file at
# the root makes every card on this machine read dirty for no reason.
/*.log

# The release box scripts/export_release.py writes. Rebuilt from the tree; never committed.
dist/
